\section{Circuit}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\input{schematic.tex}
\caption{The LDO. On chip: everything except $V_{REF}$, $I_{BIAS}$, $C_L$, $R_{ESR}$ and $I_L$.}
\label{fig:sch}
\end{figure}

\noindent The error amplifier is a five-transistor OTA with an NMOS input pair ($M_1$: $V_{REF}$, $M_2$: $V_{FB}$) and a PMOS mirror load. Its output $V_{EA}$ drives the common-source NMOS $M_{CS}$, which sinks its current from the diode-connected PMOS $M_D$. $M_D$ and the pass device $M_P$ have the same channel length and finger width, so they form a $1:@@MP_M@@$ current mirror and the gate node $V_G$ has the low impedance $1/g_{m,D}$: the pole at the large gate capacitance of $M_P$ moves to high frequency, and the output pole set by $C_L$ dominates. The bleeder $M_{BL}$ (a mirror of $M_3$) supplies a small fixed current into $V_G$, so that the subthreshold leakage of $M_{CS}$ at hot corners, multiplied by the mirror ratio, does not exceed the minimum load. The divider is made of identical \texttt{xhigh\_po} poly resistors ($w = 0.35\,\mu$m, $l = @@L_RU@@\,\mu$m each): $R_1$ = @@N_R1@@ units, $R_2$ = @@N_R2@@ units, so $\VOUT = V_{REF}(1 + R_1/R_2) = @@VOUT_IDEAL@@$\,V.

